:rsm:
# The Central Limit Theorem

:abstract:

  The Central Limit Theorem is one of the most remarkable results in probability theory. This document provides an intuitive explanation with interactive visualizations that demonstrate the theorem in action.

::

## Introduction

:definition:
:label: clt-def

The :span: {:label: clt-term} Central Limit Theorem :: (CLT) states that when independent random variables are summed, their normalized sum tends toward a normal distribution, regardless of the original distribution—provided the sample size is sufficiently large.

::

This theorem explains why the normal distribution appears so frequently in nature and science. Heights, measurement errors, test scores, and countless other phenomena follow bell curves because they result from the sum of many small, independent effects.

## The Theorem

:theorem:
:label: clt-theorem

Let $X_1, X_2, \ldots, X_n$ be independent and identically distributed random variables with mean $\mu$ and finite variance $\sigma^2$. Then as $n \to \infty$, the distribution of the standardized sample mean

$$\frac{\bar{X}_n - \mu}{\sigma / \sqrt{n}}$$

converges to the standard normal distribution $N(0, 1)$.

::

## Visualizing the CLT

The interactive figure below demonstrates the :ref:clt-term,CLT:: using dice rolls. Rolling a single die produces a uniform distribution—each outcome (1–6) is equally likely. But when we average multiple dice, something remarkable happens.

:figure:
  :path: clt-dice.html

  :caption: Distribution of sample means from dice rolls. Move the slider to increase the number of dice averaged together and watch the distribution transform from uniform to normal.

::

## Why It Works

The :ref:clt-def:: works because of how variances combine. When averaging $n$ independent observations\:

:enumerate:

  :item: The mean of the average equals the population mean $\mu$

  :item: The variance of the average is $\sigma^2 / n$

  :item: Higher-order moments become negligible as $n$ increases

::

:proof:
:label: intuition

The key insight is that extreme values become increasingly rare in averages. For a single die, rolling a 1 or 6 has probability 1/3. For the average of two dice to equal 1, *both* must show 1—probability 1/36. For 10 dice, the probability of an extreme average becomes vanishingly small, concentrating the distribution around the mean. :qed:

::

## Practical Implications

The :ref:clt-theorem:: has profound implications for statistics\:

:itemize:

  :item: **Confidence intervals** rely on sample means being approximately normal

  :item: **Hypothesis testing** uses the CLT to compute p-values

  :item: **Quality control** assumes measurement averages follow normal distributions

::

As shown in :ref:intuition::, the CLT emerges from the mathematics of combining random variables—a beautiful connection between abstract theory and practical application.

::
